-- title:
--   Driving all remaining graph-sketch layers
-- statement:
--   Let $k\ge1$ and suppose a native core state stores the first graph-sketch layer $D_1$, with a header $k-1$ and all layer, vertex, state, and predecessor working counters empty. The layer driver replaces only its previous-matrix bank by the terminal matrix:
--   $$\operatorname{Prev}_{\rm after}=\operatorname{frames}\bigl(D_k(v,a):v<n,\ a<k\bigr).$$
--   For $W=100(1+n+k)^2$, its bit time is at most $(C+13000(W+1)^2+31)(k-1)+25000(W+1)^2+1200(k+1)$, where $C=\operatorname{laterLayerCost}(n,k,|A|)$. All remaining state is restored. The driver realizes exactly the $k-1$ transitions required after graph-sketch initialization.
-- source:
--   T. Koana, Approximate Counting of k-Paths in O*(2^k) Time, STOC 2027 submission #953, October 2026, §3.2 (printed pp. 8–9 / PDF pp. 9–10) and §4.1 (printed p. 9 / PDF p. 10), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/953.pdf. Native implementation: Solutions/LayerDriver.lean, line 20; source snapshot SHA-256 664c22515d905908907133669fad06a6e8b3733350f96f837690bee1c84580e6.

